The best approach for your .NET 10 project is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's a battle-tested library that wraps all `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and `Path.*` statics behind an injectable `IFileSystem` interface.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

Looking at your `ReportGenerator`, you'd refactor it like this:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        // ...
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
        {
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);
        }

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}_{timestamp}.txt");
        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        // ...
    }
}
```

The mapping is straightforward — just prefix with `fileSystem.`:
| Before | After |
|--------|-------|
| `File.WriteAllText(...)` | `fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(...)` |
| `File.ReadAllText(...)` | `fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(...)` |
| `Directory.Exists(...)` | `fileSystem.Directory.Exists(...)` |

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports/existing.txt", new MockFileData("old report") }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
generator.GenerateReport("sales");

// Assert files were written
Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists("/tmp/reports/sales_20260709_195101.txt"));
```

> **Heads-up:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine` — those are separate static dependencies. For `DateTime`, use the built-in `TimeProvider` (register `TimeProvider.System` in DI, test with `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`). For `Environment` and `Console`, you'd need small custom wrappers. Want me to generate those too?
